WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.167
It's deeply painful

00:00:15.708 --> 00:00:19.042
to face what's happening
on our planet right now.

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:22.167
From forests burning,

00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:24.833
ocean plastic,

00:00:25.292 --> 00:00:28.518
species just gone each day,

00:00:28.542 --> 00:00:30.042
displacement.

00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:34.958
It's easy to feel totally overwhelmed.

00:00:36.208 --> 00:00:38.167
Maybe a bit helpless.

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.542
Powerless.

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:42.875
Angry.

00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:44.667
On fire.

00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:47.625
Numb.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:49.750
Disconnected.

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:52.833
Perhaps all of the above.

00:00:54.958 --> 00:00:58.434
These messy and complicated feelings,

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:00.667
they make total sense.

00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:05.708
I wish that someone
had said this to me 30 years ago.

00:01:06.792 --> 00:01:11.434
I was a college freshman
taking environmental studies,

00:01:11.458 --> 00:01:16.000
which is basically a semester
of really bad news

00:01:17.375 --> 00:01:22.018
about all the ways that humans
have profoundly damaged

00:01:22.042 --> 00:01:23.750
our beautiful earth.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:29.518
And I felt like I had been dropped
into a dark tunnel,

00:01:29.542 --> 00:01:32.059
given no tools to get out

00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:35.351
and yet expected to carry on
with my everyday life

00:01:35.375 --> 00:01:37.934
as if things were normal.

00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:40.768
But once you're exposed
to that kind of information,

00:01:40.792 --> 00:01:42.792
things are not normal anymore.

00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:48.059
And I was anxious, I was terrified,

00:01:48.083 --> 00:01:50.559
no one was talking about this,

00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.542
and I almost dropped out
of school, for real.

00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:59.434
But instead, I signed up
for a field study in California,

00:01:59.458 --> 00:02:04.393
and we were backpacking together
as a small group for two months,

00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:07.143
which I know sounds very intense.

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:12.059
And it was, but what I found
is that we talked a lot.

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:14.143
We talked about how we were feeling

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.601
about the world,

00:02:15.625 --> 00:02:16.934
openly and honestly,

00:02:16.958 --> 00:02:20.309
and no one told me at any point
to be more positive

00:02:20.333 --> 00:02:21.583
or more hopeful.

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:23.875
Not once.

00:02:24.875 --> 00:02:29.476
And surprisingly,
I found myself feeling better.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.976
I actually felt like
I could face these issues

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.851
that had seemed so insurmountable

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:37.059
more head on.

00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:39.143
And I had this epiphany:

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:42.143
What if by understanding ourselves

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:43.809
and one another,

00:02:43.833 --> 00:02:46.226
we could find our way through this crisis

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.309
in a new and different way?

00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:50.434
You know, what if psychology actually held

00:02:50.458 --> 00:02:53.101
a missing key to unlocking action

00:02:53.125 --> 00:02:57.184
on the greatest challenges
facing our planet right now?

00:02:57.208 --> 00:02:59.143
So when I got back from the field study,

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.518
I focused on clinical psychology,

00:03:01.542 --> 00:03:03.518
and I researched the relationships

00:03:03.542 --> 00:03:08.934
between trauma and grief and creativity.

00:03:08.958 --> 00:03:11.893
And the paradox at the heart of,
I think, all of this

00:03:11.917 --> 00:03:14.768
is how do we stay present

00:03:14.792 --> 00:03:17.809
with what's really painful,

00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:19.893
how do we stay connected

00:03:19.917 --> 00:03:23.958
in the face of what's threatening
and overwhelming and scary?

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.684
And it turns out that psychology
knows a lot about these things.

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:31.625
Truly, a lot.

00:03:32.292 --> 00:03:34.809
But I wasn't hearing any of this
being referenced

00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:37.309
in my environmental studies class,

00:03:37.333 --> 00:03:40.309
or the climate action meetings
I started going to,

00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:42.476
or the international conferences,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.184
where everyone is asking:

00:03:44.208 --> 00:03:48.184
Why aren't we acting faster,
and what's it going to take?

00:03:48.208 --> 00:03:51.934
And so this has become
my mission of sorts,

00:03:51.958 --> 00:03:54.851
which is that I take insights
from psychology

00:03:54.875 --> 00:03:58.143
and I translate them
into resources and tools

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:02.601
to support those working on the frontlines
to turn things around.

00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:04.518
And that means for anyone, by the way.

00:04:04.542 --> 00:04:06.851
We're all on the frontlines right now.

00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:08.143
And it's my belief,

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:10.684
after years of straddling these worlds

00:04:10.708 --> 00:04:13.309
between environment and
climate and psychology,

00:04:13.333 --> 00:04:17.268
that this actually is
a missing ingredient in our work

00:04:17.292 --> 00:04:22.143
that can exponentially accelerate
our capacities to be creative

00:04:22.167 --> 00:04:25.768
and resilient and capable
and skillful and courageous

00:04:25.792 --> 00:04:29.167
and all those things that the world
is needing from us right now.

00:04:29.833 --> 00:04:32.768
So I'm going to share
three concepts with you

00:04:32.792 --> 00:04:34.809
that I found particularly game-changing

00:04:34.833 --> 00:04:37.726
and how I make sense of this moment

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.476
for us as humans.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.726
And the first is something
called our window of tolerance.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:49.184
So Dr. Dan Siegel has described
us all as having a window.

00:04:49.208 --> 00:04:51.601
How much stress can we tolerate

00:04:51.625 --> 00:04:53.268
while staying connected

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:55.726
and what clinicians
would call "integrated."

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:57.809
Integrated, where we can actually

00:04:57.833 --> 00:04:59.893
be in touch with our thoughts and feelings

00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:02.292
and not just get kind of co-opted.

00:05:03.125 --> 00:05:05.184
And we all have a threshold.

00:05:05.208 --> 00:05:08.143
And what happens when we experience stress

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.684
beyond what we can tolerate?

00:05:10.708 --> 00:05:14.559
We tend to go into the edges
of our window.

00:05:14.583 --> 00:05:16.101
And on one hand,

00:05:16.125 --> 00:05:18.684
we might go into a sort of collapse,

00:05:18.708 --> 00:05:20.976
what's called a chaotic response,

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.143
which looks like depression, despair,

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:25.768
kind of a shutting down.

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:30.268
And on the other side of this window
is a more rigid response:

00:05:30.292 --> 00:05:31.726
denial,

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.101
anger,

00:05:33.125 --> 00:05:34.500
rigid.

00:05:35.125 --> 00:05:36.601
And so when that happens,

00:05:36.625 --> 00:05:39.309
we actually lose our capacity
to be integrated,

00:05:39.333 --> 00:05:40.684
resilient, adaptive,

00:05:40.708 --> 00:05:43.684
all those things that we want to be.

00:05:43.708 --> 00:05:45.059
And this is totally normal,

00:05:45.083 --> 00:05:49.101
but it's happening
all around the world right now, right?

00:05:49.125 --> 00:05:53.143
We're all vacillating between
these different feelings and emotions.

00:05:53.167 --> 00:05:55.851
And so with something like climate change,

00:05:55.875 --> 00:05:59.351
with every new scientific report,

00:05:59.375 --> 00:06:01.351
documentary,

00:06:01.375 --> 00:06:04.643
connecting the dots between,
you know, what we're doing

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:06.184
and the impact it's having,

00:06:06.208 --> 00:06:10.101
it can collectively be pushing us
outside of our window of tolerance.

00:06:10.125 --> 00:06:13.434
And we lose that capacity, right?

00:06:13.458 --> 00:06:16.976
So, over the years,
I've interviewed hundreds of people

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.101
from all backgrounds
and political affiliations,

00:06:20.125 --> 00:06:22.476
from the Midwest US to China,

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.351
and I talked to people
about how are we feeling

00:06:25.375 --> 00:06:26.643
about what's happening.

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:28.768
Not what opinions or beliefs.

00:06:28.792 --> 00:06:30.059
What are we feeling

00:06:30.083 --> 00:06:32.417
about what's going on
with your local environment,

00:06:32.441 --> 00:06:35.101
with your water, your soil,
the big picture.

00:06:35.125 --> 00:06:37.268
And what I hear from people

00:06:37.292 --> 00:06:39.684
almost across the board, I'm telling you,

00:06:39.708 --> 00:06:41.059
is a bind.

00:06:41.083 --> 00:06:44.893
People tell me at some point
in the conversation,

00:06:44.917 --> 00:06:47.059
"I care very deeply
about what's happening,

00:06:47.083 --> 00:06:49.434
I'm incredibly freaked out.

00:06:49.458 --> 00:06:51.184
I'm scared,

00:06:51.208 --> 00:06:54.143
I love this land, I love the birds,"

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:55.833
whatever that is,

00:06:56.792 --> 00:07:00.226
"But I feel like my actions
are insignificant.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.601
And I don't know where to start.

00:07:02.625 --> 00:07:03.893
And I'm also --"

00:07:03.917 --> 00:07:06.101
I hear between the lines
of what people say --

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:07.851
"I'm really scared to change.

00:07:07.875 --> 00:07:09.768
Really scared of any change, it's so --

00:07:09.792 --> 00:07:12.976
I can't even think about it,
it's like, unthinkable."

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.559
And this is the second concept,

00:07:14.583 --> 00:07:17.476
which is something called a double bind.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:21.976
And a double bind is when we feel
sort of like, damned if you do,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.559
and damned if you don't,

00:07:23.583 --> 00:07:26.143
and you're just kind of stuck there.

00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:29.684
It's a very intolerable human experience.

00:07:29.708 --> 00:07:33.417
And we will do anything we can
to get rid of it and just push it away.

00:07:34.417 --> 00:07:37.143
And so all that care and concern,

00:07:37.167 --> 00:07:40.601
it's there, it just goes down,
it goes underground.

00:07:40.625 --> 00:07:41.934
But what happens is,

00:07:41.958 --> 00:07:45.851
it looks like people don't care,
it looks like apathy.

00:07:45.875 --> 00:07:49.559
And so a lot of folks who are seeing
the urgency of the situation

00:07:49.583 --> 00:07:52.309
are like, "We've got to motivate you.

00:07:52.333 --> 00:07:54.934
We've got to get you psyched."

00:07:54.958 --> 00:07:57.976
And we become cheerleaders for solutions.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.667
Or like, "Here's the facts,
this is happening, wake up."

00:08:03.625 --> 00:08:06.976
And these things
are actually not inherently bad,

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.518
because we need solutions
and we need to face the facts.

00:08:10.542 --> 00:08:14.684
But inadvertently, this can backfire

00:08:14.708 --> 00:08:16.643
and lead to more numbing and inaction,

00:08:16.667 --> 00:08:19.351
which is very perplexing
for a lot of people.

00:08:19.375 --> 00:08:22.934
It's like, what the heck
is going on, right?

00:08:22.958 --> 00:08:27.476
And so, this is because of this, you know,

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:31.018
it's not really touching
what's going on underneath.

00:08:31.042 --> 00:08:34.684
So imagine that you go see a therapist,

00:08:34.708 --> 00:08:36.351
and you've got a double bind.

00:08:36.375 --> 00:08:38.393
You're feeling really stuck,

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:41.601
you know you've got to change

00:08:41.625 --> 00:08:44.059
and the therapist starts shouting at you

00:08:44.083 --> 00:08:46.976
and saying, "Don’t you see
what's happening?

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.601
If you don't act now,

00:08:48.625 --> 00:08:51.393
you're going to face
terrifying consequences.

00:08:51.417 --> 00:08:53.184
Don’t you care?

00:08:53.208 --> 00:08:54.601
What's wrong with you?

00:08:54.625 --> 00:08:56.851
What's it going to take?"

00:08:56.875 --> 00:09:00.268
Or you see a therapist
and you're feeling actually sad

00:09:00.292 --> 00:09:02.184
and grief.

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:03.559
And this therapist says,

00:09:03.583 --> 00:09:05.976
"You know, don't think about it too much.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.434
Here's some simple things you can do.

00:09:08.458 --> 00:09:09.726
Simple positive things."

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.375
And sends you on your way.

00:09:12.792 --> 00:09:14.434
So if it were me,

00:09:14.458 --> 00:09:17.333
I would fire this therapist immediately,

00:09:18.958 --> 00:09:23.726
because a good therapist
practices something called attunement.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:26.018
I love this concept so much.

00:09:26.042 --> 00:09:29.559
Attunement, right, the word "to tune."

00:09:29.583 --> 00:09:33.018
And attunement is when
we're feeling in sync,

00:09:33.042 --> 00:09:35.684
when we feel understood

00:09:35.708 --> 00:09:40.059
and we feel accepted
for exactly where we are.

00:09:40.083 --> 00:09:41.893
And we feel that, you know,

00:09:41.917 --> 00:09:44.934
we're in relationship with the world
in a way that makes sense,

00:09:44.958 --> 00:09:46.643
no one's trying to change us

00:09:46.667 --> 00:09:49.268
or shame us or judge us.

00:09:49.292 --> 00:09:50.559
Right?

00:09:50.583 --> 00:09:52.101
And attunement takes skill.

00:09:52.125 --> 00:09:53.851
When the stakes are high,

00:09:53.875 --> 00:09:59.559
let me tell you, it's very hard
to want to attune with anything,

00:09:59.583 --> 00:10:02.601
when we're facing such urgent threats.

00:10:02.625 --> 00:10:05.309
But the paradox of the moment we're in

00:10:05.333 --> 00:10:10.184
is that when we are more in tune
in our window of tolerance,

00:10:10.208 --> 00:10:14.101
we are so much more capable
of solving problems,

00:10:14.125 --> 00:10:16.976
being creative, being adaptive,

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.851
being flexible,

00:10:18.875 --> 00:10:21.083
being our brilliant selves, right?

00:10:22.708 --> 00:10:25.934
So what if our climate
and environmental work

00:10:25.958 --> 00:10:28.476
was informed by these concepts, right,

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.268
of window of tolerance,

00:10:31.292 --> 00:10:33.268
lot of double binds

00:10:33.292 --> 00:10:34.958
and attunement?

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:38.184
So it can look like a whole lot of things.

00:10:38.208 --> 00:10:39.476
So I'm asked all the time,

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:43.059
"OK, Renee, this sounds awesome
for a clinical context,

00:10:43.083 --> 00:10:44.768
we don't have time for this."

00:10:44.792 --> 00:10:47.018
And that is absolutely not true.

00:10:47.042 --> 00:10:49.934
Because we can bring attunement

00:10:49.958 --> 00:10:55.309
into every aspect
of our work on this issue.

00:10:55.333 --> 00:10:57.375
And it starts with ourselves.

00:10:58.958 --> 00:11:03.059
You actually can't do attunement
unless you're in touch with yourself,

00:11:03.083 --> 00:11:04.726
I'm sorry to break it to you.

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:06.101
There's no way around it.

00:11:06.125 --> 00:11:08.018
It's from the inside out.

00:11:08.042 --> 00:11:13.101
And so it starts with actually
tuning in to "how am I feeling?"

00:11:13.125 --> 00:11:14.684
And being compassionate.

00:11:14.708 --> 00:11:15.976
I know it's easy to say

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.477
but really being compassionate,

00:11:17.501 --> 00:11:19.559
it's like, these are hard issues.

00:11:19.583 --> 00:11:21.583
This is a hard moment to be a human being,

00:11:21.607 --> 00:11:23.143
we're waking up.

00:11:23.167 --> 00:11:25.143
I'm not a bad person.

00:11:25.167 --> 00:11:26.434
What's going on,

00:11:26.458 --> 00:11:28.768
bring curiosity into our own experience,

00:11:28.792 --> 00:11:31.434
which then allows us to attune socially,

00:11:31.458 --> 00:11:34.101
that's the next way we can apply this,

00:11:34.125 --> 00:11:36.226
is attuning, whether it's in small groups

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:37.518
or one-on-one,

00:11:37.542 --> 00:11:43.559
campaigning, strategy,
classrooms, movie theaters,

00:11:43.583 --> 00:11:44.934
parks.

00:11:44.958 --> 00:11:48.559
Where we can give each other permission
to just be who we are,

00:11:48.583 --> 00:11:50.518
and again, this allows us to move

00:11:50.542 --> 00:11:53.976
into the higher level functioning.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.018
The executive function,
the prefrontal cortex,

00:11:57.042 --> 00:12:01.268
when we feel that our
nervous system can calm down

00:12:01.292 --> 00:12:04.476
and we are understood by the other.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.226
And the third way
is leading with attunement.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:11.309
As leaders and influencers,

00:12:11.333 --> 00:12:15.226
showing up as human, as real,

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:16.518
saying, "You know what?

00:12:16.542 --> 00:12:17.958
I am really scared.

00:12:20.375 --> 00:12:22.851
I don't know what all the answers are."

00:12:22.875 --> 00:12:25.268
Can you imagine leaders saying that?

00:12:25.292 --> 00:12:26.542
"I don't know.

00:12:27.333 --> 00:12:30.226
But here we are, and we're all needed.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.559
And we're in this together.

00:12:32.583 --> 00:12:34.143
And we can do this."

00:12:34.167 --> 00:12:37.643
That's a very different message
than just, "We can do this," right.

00:12:37.667 --> 00:12:39.184
It's like, "Here we are.

00:12:39.208 --> 00:12:42.726
I'm scared, but this is happening."

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:45.101
So here's the thing,

00:12:45.125 --> 00:12:46.518
all of this work exists,

00:12:46.542 --> 00:12:50.393
we have the tools
to create these conditions

00:12:50.417 --> 00:12:53.893
that can allow us to show up

00:12:53.917 --> 00:12:56.893
as our brilliant selves.

00:12:56.917 --> 00:13:00.601
And I know, without doubt, 100 percent,

00:13:00.625 --> 00:13:04.018
that each one of us has the capacity

00:13:04.042 --> 00:13:06.476
to meet these challenges
with the ingenuity

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:10.268
and brilliance and bravery
that we as humans have.

00:13:10.292 --> 00:13:13.684
We just need to cultivate
the conditions together.

00:13:13.708 --> 00:13:15.559
We need each other.

00:13:15.583 --> 00:13:17.434
To support each other

00:13:17.458 --> 00:13:20.434
and allow ourselves to really meet this.

00:13:20.458 --> 00:13:22.292
That's what we need, so ...

00:13:23.625 --> 00:13:25.250
Let's take a deep breath.

00:13:27.333 --> 00:13:29.559
Have compassion for ourselves

00:13:29.583 --> 00:13:32.976
and one another in this moment,
time in history,

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.893
so we collectively process
these painful truths,

00:13:36.917 --> 00:13:38.875
these difficult realities.

00:13:40.333 --> 00:13:41.875
Let's do this together.

00:13:42.958 --> 00:13:46.917
The world is ready for us to do this.

00:13:47.875 --> 00:13:49.708
And we can do this.

00:13:50.958 --> 00:13:52.226
Thank you.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:56.375
(Applause)

